'Cause I had to sidebar.

9
00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.590
I know you tweeted about "Don't Look Up."

10
00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:22.870
So when I saw "Don't Look Up,"

11
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.423
I watched it at night before bed alone.

12
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.900
Big mistake.

13
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.500
You should watch it with friends during the daytime.

14
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.530
Yeah.

15
00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:36.060
But the next day, the movie really resonated with me,

16
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.710
and I had a very different feeling

17
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.150
about it kind of in processing it.

18
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.700
What do you think of the film?

19
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.410
You were tweeting about it.

20
00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.620
Yeah, I saw it, like everybody else.

21
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.160 line:15%
And it's comical, and playful, and everything.

22
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.300 line:15%
And then, "I said, wait a minute.

23
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.920 line:15%
That's the experience I had on social media.

24
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.950 line:15%
That's my experience I had with the news

25
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.440
and with politicians.

26
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.950
And I started assembling the puzzle pieces.

27
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.280
And I said to myself, "This isn't just some story.

28
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.970
This is a documentary of what would happen

29
00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.280
if we find a killer asteroid."

30
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.360
I don't know that I have confidence

31
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:20.860
that we have the collective, societal maturity

32
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.690
to handle that information,

33
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.960
and to save civilization

34
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.270
that we have so long,

35
00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:33.670
hard built as part of what it is to be alive

36
00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.520
in this world as human beings.

37
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.890
So yeah, it scared me to death too.

38
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.160
Mm.
Oh yeah.

39
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.320
Yeah.
And I'll tell you just,

40
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.990
I mean, think of the global warming analog to that.

41
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:44.990
All right?
Yes.

42
00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:48.010
Almost every important city in the world

43
00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.260
was built on the water's edge,

44
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.960
be it a lake, a river or the ocean,

45
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.720
to enable commerce, transportation, irrigation.

46
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.270
Do you realize if we lose

47
00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:02.230 line:15%
the Greenland and Antarctic ice caps,

48
00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.190 line:15%
